text:
{  
  "distractors": [  
    {  
      "text": "It shows that the Repair Café has failed to change anyone’s behavior regarding waste or repair.",  
      "why_wrong": "The text explicitly states that over three hundred items have been repaired and that the café has changed how many residents think about repairing rather than discarding things—directly contradicting the idea of no behavioral change."  
    },  
    {  
      "text": "It demonstrates that the café has caused significant financial harm to local businesses like Ellis & Co.",  
      "why_wrong": "The text presents Mrs. Ellis’s concern as a minority view and shows the council’s response as a compromise, not evidence of actual harm; the café is framed as beneficial overall, not damaging to local shops."  
    },  
    {  
      "text": "It proves that the Repair Café has become a major source of income for the town council.",  
      "why_wrong": "The text never mentions the café generating income for the council; instead, it focuses on environmental and social benefits, and the council’s role is advisory and mediating, not financial."  
    },  
    {  
      "text": "It clearly shows that the café has united the entire town in support of a single solution to waste.",  
      "why_wrong": "The text states that community members are divided—some support the café, others worry about small businesses—so it does not show unanimous agreement, contradicting the claim of full unity."  
    },  
    {  
      "text": "It establishes that repairing items is now more expensive than buying new ones in Millbrook.",  
      "why_wrong": "The text emphasizes that the café helps people save money, and no evidence is given that repairs are more costly; the focus is on affordability and waste reduction, not increased expense."  
    },  
    {  
      "text": "It illustrates that the Repair Café has replaced the need for local shops like Ellis & Co.",  
      "why_wrong": "The text suggests a collaborative solution—promoting the shop and offering discounts on parts—indicating the café complements rather than replaces local businesses, directly contradicting replacement."  
    }  
  ]  
}
